  "headline": "Analysts suggest Israel's upcoming election may not alter Iran war policy",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Regional policymakers and analysts told Arab News that Israel’s election, scheduled for October 20, 2026, may change the country's leader but is unlikely to shift its core strategy regarding Iran. According to these analysts, Iran is expected to remain Israel's chief adversary, military force will continue to be a preferred tool, and Palestinian statehood remains a distant prospect. They suggest the strategic assumptions underpinning Israeli policy were hardened by the October 7, 2023, attacks on southern Israeli communities by Hamas and subsequent wars in Gaza, Lebanon, and Iran. Analysts noted that the real divide between political factions may be one of degree rather than direction, specifically regarding the aggressiveness of warfare, the expansion of Jewish settlements in the West Bank, and the use of diplomacy. Israel’s current security doctrine is described as resting on three pillars: breaking Iran, holding buffer zones beyond its borders, and using force to deter enemies."
    },
    {
      "text": "The election pits Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s right-wing Likud party and its ultranationalist allies against an opposition led by Gadi Eisenkot, a former military chief, and his Yashar party. Analysts suggested that a victory for Netanyahu, supported by far-right coalition partners, could potentially embolden him to increase the war with Iran, tighten control over the occupied West Bank, and move diplomacy further to the margins. Aviv Bushinsky, a former adviser to Netanyahu, stated that if Netanyahu is elected, settlement expansion and wars against Iran, Hamas, and Hezbollah will continue, potentially even more aggressively. On October 18, 2026, Netanyahu said Israel must prioritize prevention and offense over containment and defense, including pre-emptive strikes to thwart emerging threats, while maximizing independence in armaments and establishing Israel as a regional power."
    }
  ]
}

